    <h1 style="margin-top: 4rem; font-size: 2.5rem; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--w-color-primary-50); padding-bottom: 0.5rem; margin-bottom: 2rem;">Conclusion</h1>
    
    <div style="font-size: 1.15rem; line-height: 1.7; color: var(--w-color-primary-20);">
        <p>When Parallax Tide was banned, the consensus was that Replenish was dead. The results seem to tell a different story.</p>

        <p>The matchup spread is strong. Replenish dominates midrange and creature strategies, has an excellent Enchantress matchup, and is favored against most control decks. The weaknesses are specific: fast combo like Devourer, and decks that can combine a clock with disruption.</p>

        <p>Decree of Silence proved more than a replacement. Meddling Mage migrated from sideboard to maindeck. The deck’s identity shifted from pure combo toward a balanced hybrid that rewards patience, resource management, and metagame awareness.</p>

        <p>Replenish is not the underdog anyone expected. It remains one of Premodern’s premier archetypes - a puzzle where every match starts with pieces in a different arrangement, and the joy is in figuring out the path to victory. If you figure out the puzzle, you’re likely to win. And that’s all any of us are really trying to do.</p>
        
        <p style="margin-top: 2rem; font-style: italic; color: var(--w-color-primary-30); text-align: right;">
            — Brian Siu, Squee’s House of Pizza
        </p>
    </div>

    </Layout>
